## so a small disagreement is normal. Half the player's own height is not.
const STAND_UNDER_TOLERANCE := 7.0
## How far a placed object may sit off the ground under it before it counts
## as floating. Real scenery is authored a little into or above the terrain
## and a small gap is normal; a player-height gap is not.
const FLOATING_TOLERANCE := 12.0
## How many offending objects the float report lists. Enough to group them
## by model and see whether an offset is a property of the model, of the
## region, or of neither.
const QA_FLOAT_MAX_ROWS := 200

## Real region streaming (2026-08-24): the original fixed 3x3 grid only
## ever loaded once, centered on wherever a zone's initial spawn/entry
## point was -- a player who kept walking in one direction would
## eventually walk off the edge of loaded terrain into the placeholder
## void, since nothing re-triggered loading as they moved. These track
## enough state for _maybe_stream_regions() (called every
## _physics_process) to load/unload real region cells as the player
## actually moves, keeping a real REGION_LOAD_RADIUS window centered on
## wherever they are, not just where they started.
##
## _grid_origin_region/_grid_origin_world anchor the mapping between real
## region coordinates and Godot world space: _grid_origin_world is the
## world position the real (dx=0, dy=0) cell's own terrain_group stands at
## (or would stand at) -- every real cell's world position is
## `_grid_origin_world + RegionGridState.cell_world_offset(dx, dy)` by
## construction (see _load_region_cell and _group_position). Until round 571
## it was read off the centre group once it had loaded; since then it is
## computed from the grid's anchor (_frame_corner), because the centre's own
## terrain can fail to load and the corner is the same either way. This
## mapping stays fixed for a zone's whole lifetime, even as cells stream
## in/out around it, so already-placed nodes never need to move.
##
## Since round 570 the origin is the coordinate FRAME the server names on the
## wire (FrameRegion; _frame_coords), and _grid_centre_region is the region
## the grid is built around (the zone's own, _resolve_zone_region). They are
## the same region for every zone unless the server runs -single-frame (round
## 571, default off). When they are the same, _grid_origin_world is where the
## centre group stands; when they differ the centre is the cell (centre -
## frame) and _grid_origin_world the frame region's corner, where no group
## need stand (RegionGridState's "THE COORDINATE FRAME").
var _grid_origin_region := Vector2i.ZERO
var _grid_centre_region := Vector2i.ZERO
var _grid_origin_world := Vector3.ZERO
var _grid_shared_anchor_height := NAN
var _grid_has_origin := false
## The per-region bookkeeping of the loaded grid -- the loaded and queued
## cells ("dx_dy", relative to _grid_origin_region), the NPC marks, the nav
## graphs and the navmesh / heightmap / object-collision caches -- in one
## object, so the streaming unload forgets a region whole
## (RegionGridState.unload, round 563) and a zone change clears it whole
## (clear_all). Per instance: a reconnect's second World3D starts empty.
var _grid := RegionGridState.new()
var _last_stream_cell := Vector2i(0x7fffffff, 0x7fffffff) # sentinel so the first real check always runs
# Region cells waiting to be built, streamed a few per frame instead of a
# whole leading row in one frame -- that synchronous row was the ~1s freeze
# on every boundary crossing (a playtest complaint). The cell underfoot is
# always already loaded (it was a neighbour before the crossing), so the
# queued cells are ahead of the player and can arrive over the next frames.
var _load_queue: Array = [] # of {rx, ry, dx, dy, anchor, key}; keys in _grid.queued_cells
const STREAM_CELLS_PER_FRAME := 1
## At most this many streamed cells prepare their terrain on WorkerThreadPool
## tasks at once (round 565; see _terrain_prepare) -- and, since round 566,
## their objects in the same task (_cell_prepare). An OWN TUNING CHOICE, not
## a value from the game or the engine: two let one cell prepare while the
## previous one commits, which is all a budget of one commit a frame
## (STREAM_CELLS_PER_FRAME) can use, without more tasks queueing behind
## Pk2Reader's lock. The benchmark (client/tools/README.md, "Load
## benchmark") is what it is judged by.
const TERRAIN_JOBS_IN_FLIGHT := 2
## Streamed cells whose terrain (and, since round 566, objects) is preparing
## on a WorkerThreadPool task (round 565), oldest first, each {"task": the
## pool's task id, "cell": its _load_queue entry, "job": the job Dictionary
## the task fills}. The task id
## is kept here and not in the job, because the task writes the job while it
## runs. A cell keeps its _grid.queued_cells mark until it is committed or
## dropped, so _maybe_stream_regions does not queue it a second time while
## it prepares.
var _cell_jobs: Array = []
## True while _drain_initial_load pumps the cell jobs itself (a world entry,
## a zone change), so the physics step's _process_stream_queue leaves them
## alone: one owner of the dispatch and of the commit budget at a time. Until
## round 565 both built from the same _load_queue in the same frame -- the
## drain's two cells plus the physics step's one (round 561).
var _drain_pumping := false
# The "NOW LOADING" screen shown to a real player during world entry (never a
# QA render, whose synchronous load a screenshot depends on).
var _loading_screen: LoadingScreen
## True once _drain_initial_load has finished this instance's entry (either
## path). The QA shots wait on it: under the loading-screen drain a shot
## armed at tick 0 printed its frame during the sign-in, 160 log lines
## before `region grid loaded` (round 558's first run), so the frame it named
## was of no world at all.
var _initial_load_done := false
## Where this world's load time goes -- per cell, per phase and per frame
## (round 561, LoadTimings; the benchmark it feeds is client/tools/README.md's
## "Load benchmark"). Per instance, like every other cache here. Its grid
## totals are zeroed where a drain's clock starts (_ready,
## _on_change_zone_result): until this round the three accumulators here
## were never reset, so a second drain's `region grid loaded` split summed
## both drains (round 559's review).
var _load_timings := LoadTimings.new()

